      "batched_8_measured_ms": 1305.3,
      "answer": "Batching is a legitimate model ONLY for a dedicated single-request server that can hold 8 concurrent sequences; it converts idle 8-wide throughput into latency. It is measured here and IS ~3.4x faster than drawing sequentially, so batching is the right choice IF you are doing best-of-8 at all. What is NOT legitimate is the claim that batching makes N free: measured batch-8 costs 689 ms vs 350 ms for a single greedy draw (2.0x), and the whole best-of-8 round-trip is 1305 ms vs a 665 ms single 32B forward. Under ANY serving assumption (sequential 4442 ms or batched 1305 ms) the open-text best-of-8 arm is SLOWER than simply calling the 32B once. In a real multi-tenant server the batched figure is also optimistic, because the 8-wide slots are not free \u2014 they displace other requests."
